    <details class="story"><summary>An evening at the Duomo <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="m6 9 6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary>
          <div class="tale"><p>One evening, sitting outside the Duomo in Florence, I noticed a stranger next to me. Christopher, 90, was a woodworker who frequented the bench on warm evenings. He was grumpy, and I eventually got him to give me full-sentence answers. We were in no way the same, but we were both there, on a warm evening, admiring the Duomo.</p>
          <p>That is the answer I keep coming back to: a story is told through shared human experiences, wants, and needs. The power lies in naming the experience. For this one, it was Gemütlichkeit, a feeling of warmth, friendliness, and good cheer.</p></div>
